.TH FIND 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
find \- walk a directory tree and print matching paths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [\-name " pattern" ]
.RI [\-type " f|d|l" ]
.RI [\-maxdepth " n" ]
.RI [\-mindepth " n" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Recursively walk each
.I path
(default
.BR . )
and print every entry that matches all given filters. With no filters, prints every entry under
.IR path .
.TP
.BI \-name " pattern"
Match the entry's base name against a shell glob:
.B *
matches any run of characters (including none),
.B ?
matches exactly one. No character classes (e.g.
.BR [abc] ).
.TP
.BI \-type " f|d|l"
Only regular files, directories, or symlinks respectively.
.TP
.BI \-maxdepth " n" ", " \-mindepth " n"
Limit how far below each starting
.I path
an entry may be;
.I path
itself is depth 0.
.SH NOTES
.BR \-exec ", " \-size ", " \-mtime / \-newer ,
and
.B [abc]
character classes are not implemented -- documented gaps, not silent ones; see mitos\-utils\-compat(7). Traversal skips symlinked directories rather than following them, so a symlink cycle can't cause an infinite walk.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR, and \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if any path couldn't be read, 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
